import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.DYPTBNgj.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chat with a Local Model","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/chat-with-a-local-model.md","filePath":"guide/chat-with-a-local-model.md"}'),r={name:"guide/chat-with-a-local-model.md"};function s(d,e,i,l,h,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="chat-with-a-local-model" tabindex="-1">Chat with a Local Model <a class="header-anchor" href="#chat-with-a-local-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chat with a Local Model&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page builds a small workflow and opens the <a href="./chat-panel">Chat Panel</a> on it, so you can talk to a model running on your own machine from inside Aerini. Nothing you type leaves your computer.</p><p><strong>Before you start:</strong> Ollama is installed, you&#39;ve pulled a model, and AI Prompt already answers from it. If not, do the <a href="./local-models-quickstart">Local Models Quickstart</a> first. You also need the desktop app; the Chat Panel doesn&#39;t exist in <code>aerini-server</code>.</p><h2 id="_1-build-the-workflow" tabindex="-1">1. Build the workflow <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-build-the-workflow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Build the workflow&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Chat needs a <strong>Webhook</strong> node to receive your message and an <strong>Output</strong> node to send the reply back. Put an <strong>AI Prompt</strong> between them and connect the three in order: Webhook, AI Prompt, Output.</p><p><strong>Webhook.</strong> Leave the defaults (port <code>3456</code>, path <code>/webhook</code>). The Chat Panel sends your message to this node on your own machine, so there&#39;s no secret or token to set.</p><p><strong>AI Prompt:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Provider</strong></td><td><code>local</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Base URL</strong></td><td><code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code> (the <strong>Use Ollama defaults</strong> button fills it in)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td>The exact tag you pulled, for example <code>llama3.2</code>. <strong>Fetch Models</strong> lists what&#39;s installed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prompt</strong></td><td><code>{{Webhook.output.body.message}}</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>System</strong></td><td>Optional. For example: <code>You are a concise, friendly assistant.</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Output.</strong> Set <strong>Field</strong> to <code>content</code>. Output shows whatever reaches it, and AI Prompt returns an object, so without this the Chat Panel would pretty-print the whole object as JSON instead of showing just the reply text.</p><p>Save with <code>Ctrl+S</code> (<code>Cmd+S</code> on macOS).</p><h2 id="_2-open-chat-and-start-the-workflow" tabindex="-1">2. Open Chat and start the workflow <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-open-chat-and-start-the-workflow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Open Chat and start the workflow&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Chat</strong> button in the toolbar appears once the canvas has a Webhook and an Output node. Click it, or press <code>Ctrl+Shift+C</code> (<code>Cmd+Shift+C</code> on macOS).</p><p>The workflow has to be running in the background before it can reply. A notice in the panel has a <strong>Start</strong> button. Click it. It does the same as the toolbar&#39;s <strong>Schedule Run</strong>: it saves the workflow if needed and hands it to the scheduler. If port <code>3456</code> is taken, Chat tries the next few ports and tells you which one it used.</p><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p>Don&#39;t use the plain <strong>Run</strong> button with a Webhook trigger. It waits for one request that Chat can never send. If you press it, Aerini offers <strong>Start Instead</strong> (Chat open) or <strong>Open Chat</strong> (Chat closed).</p></div><h2 id="_3-send-a-message" tabindex="-1">3. Send a message <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-send-a-message" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Send a message&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Type a message and press <strong>Enter</strong> (<code>Shift+Enter</code> for a new line). You should see a typing indicator, and then the reply appears all at once. Replies don&#39;t stream in word by word.</p><p>Local models can be slow, especially on the first message while Ollama loads the model. After 30 seconds the panel adds a note that the model is still working. That&#39;s not a failure: Chat keeps waiting and keeps the input locked until the reply arrives or the run fails. To give up on a reply, click <strong>Stop</strong>; the workflow keeps running and your next message works without a restart.</p><p>The AI Prompt node itself waits up to an hour by default. If you have slow hardware and want a different limit, change <strong>Timeout (seconds)</strong> on the node.</p><h2 id="_4-make-it-remember-the-conversation" tabindex="-1">4. Make it remember the conversation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-make-it-remember-the-conversation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Make it remember the conversation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Right now every message starts fresh, because AI Prompt only sees the current message. To give the model memory, add an <strong>AI Memory</strong> node:</p><ol><li>Add an AI Memory node with <strong>Operation</strong> <code>read</code>, <strong>Session ID</strong> <code>{{Webhook.output.body.session_id}}</code> and <strong>Max Messages</strong> <code>20</code>, between Webhook and AI Prompt.</li><li>Change the AI Prompt <strong>Prompt</strong> to include the history, for example: <code>Conversation so far (newest first): {{AI Memory (read).output.messages}}</code> followed by a new line and <code>User: {{Webhook.output.body.message}}</code>.</li><li>After AI Prompt, add two AI Memory nodes with <strong>Operation</strong> <code>append</code> and the same Session ID: one with <strong>Role</strong> <code>user</code> and <strong>Content</strong> <code>{{Webhook.output.body.message}}</code>, then one with <strong>Role</strong> <code>assistant</code> and <strong>Content</strong> <code>{{AI Prompt.output.content}}</code>, chained one after the other.</li><li>Wire <strong>Output</strong> directly from AI Prompt, in parallel with the append branch, and set Output&#39;s <strong>Source Node</strong> to <code>AI Prompt</code> and <strong>Field</strong> to <code>content</code>. With a blank Source Node, Output falls back to whichever node finished most recently, which can be an append node, and the panel would then show its output instead of the reply.</li></ol><p>The complete wiring, including why it takes two appends, is in the <a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/Chatbot___AI_Memory___Widget.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chatbot with AI Memory and Widget example</a>. The Chat Panel&#39;s <strong>Clear</strong> button and session delete both clear the matching AI Memory session, because they use the same session ID.</p><div class="warning custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">WARNING</p><p>History grows with every turn, and Ollama&#39;s context window is limited. Current Ollama documentation gives a default of 4096 tokens, and older versions used 2048. When the history no longer fits, the model can lose the start of the conversation without any error. Keep <strong>Max Messages</strong> modest, or raise Ollama&#39;s limit with <code>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH</code>.</p></div><h2 id="if-it-didn-t-work" tabindex="-1">If it didn&#39;t work <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-it-didn-t-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If it didn&#39;t work&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No <strong>Chat</strong> button</td><td>The canvas needs both a Webhook and an Output node.</td></tr><tr><td>The reply is a block of JSON</td><td>Set the Output node&#39;s <strong>Field</strong> to <code>content</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>A red error with <strong>Retry</strong></td><td>The run failed. Open the run in <a href="./background-runs">Background Runs</a> to see which node failed. A network error on AI Prompt usually means Ollama isn&#39;t running.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Restart &amp; Retry</strong> appears</td><td>The background job stopped. This button restarts it and resends.</td></tr><tr><td>Messages are ignored while a reply is pending</td><td>The workflow handles one message at a time, which is why the input stays locked.</td></tr><tr><td><code>model is required when provider is &quot;local&quot;</code></td><td>Model is blank on AI Prompt.</td></tr></tbody></table><details class="details custom-block"><summary>For experienced users</summary><ul><li>Chat posts <code>{&quot;message&quot;: &quot;...&quot;, &quot;session_id&quot;: &quot;...&quot;}</code> to the workflow&#39;s own Webhook listener on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, plus <code>attachments</code> when files are attached. The reply is read from the <strong>Output</strong> node&#39;s result in run history, not from the HTTP response.</li><li>Attachments: files reach the model only if a node wires them in, for example <code>{{Webhook.output.files}}</code> into AI Prompt&#39;s <strong>Files</strong> port. The panel offers to make that connection for you. A local model can only use an image if it supports images, and PDFs are sent as <code>file</code> blocks that a local or third-party endpoint may reject. Text and markdown files are decoded into the system prompt for every provider.</li><li>Several Webhook or Output nodes: Chat uses whichever it finds first while scanning the workflow.</li><li>Tool calling with the <strong>AI Agent</strong> node depends on the specific model, not just the provider. See <a href="./local-models#ai-agent">Local Models, AI Agent</a>.</li><li>Per-workflow chat settings (attachments, image replies, message length) live under <strong>Workflow Settings</strong>; see <a href="./chat-panel#configuring-chat">Chat Panel, Configuring Chat</a>.</li></ul></details>',26)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
